{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1792710482455,"sessionID":"ses_e9a22f168ffeMitJv1wz5wxyzc","part":{"id":"prt_165ddad18001nE4fn3VIurNQD1","sessionID":"ses_e9a22f168ffeMitJv1wz5wxyzc","messageID":"msg_165dda9d6001SfTB1b4pU1CxQc","type":"reasoning","text":"I'm examining the key details of this case—a debt collection lawsuit in Campinas with two defendants represented by different law firms, where the citation was notified via the electronic journal. I'm working through whether the defendant qualifies for doubled deadlines under the relevant CPC statute.\n\nThe critical distinction is between the citation phase and the sentencing phase. For the citation, doubled deadlines apply because it's a material notification to two defendants with separate counsel in different firms, but the statute prohibits doubled deadlines in electronic case files—so the citation actually gets the standard 15-day period. When the sentence is published via the electronic journal, the deadline count begins from the first business day after that publication.\n\nNow I'm working through the exact dates to determine when the deadline actually falls, considering the publication date, the counting rules, and whether any holidays or suspensions interfere with the calculation. I see the issue now—when the deadline of 15 days is counted from October 22nd, and October 28th is a holiday with no forense proceedings in Campinas, the start day should be pushed forward to October 29th according to Article 224 §1. But that creates a chicken-and-egg problem: the deadline for the start day isn't known until I've done the count, yet the count depends on knowing the start day. I need to think through this iteratively—either count from October 22nd, and if the resulting deadline lands on a holiday, push it forward; or account for the fact that the start day itself might be pushed, which shifts everything downstream. Now I'm counting the business days for the contestação deadline, skipping weekends and the Day of the Serviced holiday on October 28th, which lands on November 5th. Then I need to verify this by working backward—counting 15 business days back from that deadline, excluding the deadline day itself but including the start day, making sure the count is accurate.\n\nBut I'm realizing the deadline could also be subject to prorrogação under the same rule if it falls on a non-business day, so I should double-check whether November 5th actually qualifies as a proper business day for filing, and if not, I'll need to push it forward further. I think the cleanest interpretation is to push the start date forward to the next business day, then count the 15 days according to Article 219, and verify that the final deadline doesn't land on a non-business day—but that creates a potential infinite loop, so the more standard approach is to follow the article literally first, then check whether the deadline needs adjustment. I need to reconsider the exact wording of Article 224 §1. Looking at the actual text, \"dias\" here refers to calendar days, not business days, so when October 19th falls on a Saturday, the next business day would be Monday the 21st. But I should clarify what the article actually requires and whether this prorrogation rule applies to the publication date or the deadline itself. I'm settling on the clean interpretation: the citation deadline count should start October 21st (the next business day after the Saturday), then I'll work through each rule's plain reading while flagging where the protraction question creates ambiguity. I'm realizing the key distinction: Article 231 says the deadline start is the publication date itself, but Articles 224 §1 and 219 modify this by requiring the first business day if that publication date falls on a non-business day. So for citation, the deadline properly starts October 21st, while for the sentence in December, I need to verify whether December 10th counts as having a forense session or if it also gets protracted. Now I'm confirming that December 15th falls within the recess period, so it gets pushed forward. The calculation shows December 15th is a Sunday (a holiday), which means the deadline extends to Monday December 16th, then further to December 17th since December 17th is also a holiday. December 17th is a Thursday with a scheduled session, so that's when embargos de declaração are due—5 business days from December 21st. For the apelacao deadline of 15 business days, I'm counting weekdays from December 21st, skipping weekends and holidays, which lands on January 6th, 2027.\n\nBut there's a complication: January 1st through 5th might fall within the recess period, which typically runs through January 20th. Since January 6th is after January 20th doesn't hold up—January 6th comes before January 20th, so it's actually inside the recess. This means the deadline gets suspended and needs to be protracted further. Now I'm working through the embargos deadline more carefully—if they're filed on December 17th within the recess period, they'd interrupt the appeal deadline, but the appeal itself needs a new calculation. So I've got the citation deadlines locked in: contestação due November 5th. For the sentence phase, embargos are due December 17th, and the apelacao deadline lands on January 8th, 2027 after accounting for the recess suspending the count and protracting past the holiday.\n\nNow checking whether the doubled deadline rule applies to the sentence phase—it doesn't, because Article 229 §2 prohibits doubled deadlines in electronic case files, which is exactly what this is. So both Ferreira & Lima and Logística Campos get the standard 15-day period for the sentence.\n\nThe citation deadline was actually also affected: even though Article 229 caput could have granted the doubled deadline, §2 again bars it in electronic autos, so that doesn't apply either. The client's assumption on doubled deadline appears to be incorrect.\n\nI'm also noting one remaining uncertainty: the exact deadline for contestação depends on whether the starting day itself gets protracted. The conservative reading—skipping October 19th since it's a Saturday and starting from the 21st—would push the deadline to November 5th, which differs from the simpler calculation of November 3rd that ignores this nuance.\n\nThe core question is whether Article 231's \"data de publicação\" refers strictly to the publication date or the adjusted start of the deadline. If it's the latter, then Article 224 §1 applies and October 19th gets protracted to October 21st, pushing everything forward. If it's the former, the counting begins October 19th regardless of the weekday. The conservative interpretation—where the start gets protracted to the next business day—would yield the first business day as October 21st. I'll use the more conservative November 5th deadline but flag the ambiguity in my analysis, and I can note this as a question for clarification in the STATUS.md document.\n\nNow I'm double-checking the recess dates to confirm they fall within the specified window and don't interfere with the deadline. I'm seeing that the holiday on October 28th falls outside the recess period, so it still affects the counting. The recess runs from December 20th to January 20th, so my calculations hold—the recess period includes everything from that point through mid-January. Now I need to verify whether December 25th, which is a Christmas holiday during the recess, should be counted separately.\n\nFor the embargos deadline on December 17th, that's right before the recess begins, so it should still be valid. If someone files embargos during the recess period though, there's a question of whether that interrupts the appeal deadline or gets suspended under Article 220—this is a genuine ambiguity worth noting. The same issue applies to the citation deadline of November 5th and the embargos deadline itself.\n\nLet me finalize the calculations and structure the deliverables: I'll write out the prazos.json file with the computed dates, create the memory.md file with the day-by-day breakdown and citation references, and draft the questions for the client to clarify the recess and interruption rules.\n\nI also need to reconsider the doubled deadline issue—the client believes they qualify for deadline doubling based on having two defendants with different lawyers under Art. 229 caput, but Art. 229 §2 makes this inapplicable in electronic case files, so the answer is no double deadline. This is actually a substantive finding that resolves the client's core question, even though the contract suggested it might be true.\n\nNow I'm carefully working through the exact deadlines, starting with the citation publication date and the citation deadline. Now I'm recalculating the deadlines more carefully, working through the calendar to count the 15 business days. The publication was released on October 19th, which was a Saturday, so the actual publication date counts as Monday October 21st. From there, I'm tracking the business days forward, skipping weekends and the October 28th holiday, to determine when the deadline actually falls. I'm settling on November 5th for the contestação deadline, treating the protraction of October 19th to the 21st as part of how deadlines are calculated—this is the more conservative approach and likely what the client needs. Now I'm working through the sentenca deadline, starting with a publication date of December 10th and counting forward 15 business days while excluding holidays and the December 20th-20th recess period. December 18th is Friday, so the embargos vencimento falls on December 17th. But I'm second-guessing whether the start date should be protracted—checking December 10th against the calendar, it's a Thursday with a normal session, so no protraction needed there. Let me stick with December 10th as the publication date and count 5 business days from December 11th. Now I'm finishing that business day count to get 15 days, working through early December and accounting for the Christmas holiday on December 25th. Then I'm calculating the appeal deadline starting from January 6th, accounting for the fact that January 1st through 5th are holidays within the recess period, which pushes the deadline forward to January 8th, 2027. I'm also noting the suspended days that were considered in the calculation.\n\nNow I'm documenting the key issues: the client's mistaken belief that they get doubled deadlines doesn't apply here since it's an electronic case, and I need to flag the ambiguity in contestação about whether the start date should be protracted or counted from the 19th. I'm also pointing out the risk that if embargos de declaração are filed during the recess period, they might suspend the appeal deadline per Article 1026. The client specifically wants the contestação dates plus the appeal dates after the sentence came out.\n\nFor the questions to the client, I need to verify a few things: confirm the citation date of October 19, 2026, and the sentence publication date of December 10, 2026; clarify whether both defendants will actually file separate defenses so the doubled deadline logic applies; and confirm whether embargos de declaração will be filed and when. I should also verify the current phase—since the sentence has already been issued, this is getting more complex than a standard citation matter.\n","metadata":{"reasoningField":"reasoning"},"time":{"start":1792710389016,"end":1792710482455}}}
