{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1792711000357,"sessionID":"ses_e9a22f168ffeMitJv1wz5wxyzc","part":{"id":"prt_165e4cc8f0012jypUZffSpj3h4","sessionID":"ses_e9a22f168ffeMitJv1wz5wxyzc","messageID":"msg_165e4bc7c001qlOErwFeV7bv4n","type":"reasoning","text":"Wait, I'm making a mess. Let me think carefully about the appeal date more precisely.\n\nThe issue is I have conflicting numbers. Let me think about the actual correct appeal vencimento.\n\nI have the Python result: contagem = ['2026-12-10(Thu)', ..., 15 days, vencimento 2027-02-01 Mon]. But then I also wrote 02/02/2027 in some places and 10/01/2027 in others and the table shows 10/01.\n\nThis is genuinely inconsistent. Let me recompute VERY carefully by hand-tracing the Python.\n\nPython add_bdays(11/12, 15) gives 15 business days starting from 11/12 inclusive, excluding weekends, holidays {28/11, 24/12, 31/12}, and recess [20/12, 20/01].\n\nResult listed: 2026-12-10(Thu), 11/12(Fri), 14(Mon), 15, 16, 17, 18(Fri), 2027-01-21(Thu)... \n\nWait — the Python output shows day 1 = 12/10 (Thursday). But the contagem starts at 11/12 (the \"início contagem\" = 11/12). And 12/10 is Thursday, 11/12 is Friday. So the Python started counting at 10/12, not 11/12!\n\nWait no. Let me re-read. The Python output for apelacao says:\ncontagem: ['2026-12-10(Thu)', '2026-12-11(Fri)', '2026-12-14(Mon)', ...]\n\nSo it started at 10/12 (Thursday). That means my call added_bdays(10/12, 15)? No, I called add_bdays(sen_inicio, 15) where sen_inicio = protract(10/12). protract(10/12) = 10/12 itself (10/12 is Thursday, a business day, not a holiday, not in recess). So sen_inicio = 10/12! Not 11/12.\n\nWait, in my \"corrected\" Python I defined sen_inicio=protract(datetime.date(2026,12,10)). protract checks: weekday>=5? No (Thursday=3). in bd? No. in recess? recess is 20/12..20/01. 10/12 < 20/12, so not in recess. So returns 10/12. So sen_inicio = 10/12/2026.\n\nBut for CITACAO I called protract(19/10) and got back 19/10 too (10/19 Monday, business, not holiday, not recess). So the inicio_contagem = 19/10. That matches JSON.\n\nHmm, but for the sentenca I wrote inicio_contagem = 2026-12-11 in JSON and memoria. But Python said inicio = 10/12 (protract returns 10/12). Contradiction!\n\nLet me reconcile. The issue: what is \"início contagem\"?\n\nFor CPC art. 224 §3º: \"A contagem do prazo terá início no primeiro dia útil que seguir ao da publicação\" = the FIRST BUSINESS DAY FOLLOWING THE PUBLICATION.\n\nPublication = 10/12/2026 (Thursday, business day). The first business day FOLLOWING 10/12 = 11/12 (Friday). So inicio_contagem = 11/12/2026. \n\nBut my Python protract(10/12) returned 10/12 because protract just returns the input if it's already a business day (it doesn't add one). protract is only for protraÇÃO (when the date falls on a non-business day, move forward). It does NOT implement \"first day following.\"\n\nSo for iníco_contagem I need: first business day AFTER publicação = publicação + (advance if needed to next business day). \n\nPublication 10/12 (Thu, business). Follow by: 11/12 (Fri, business) → início = 11/12. ✓ (matches what I wrote in JSON)\nPublication 19/10 (Mon, business). Follow by: 20/10 (Tue, business) → início = 20/10.\n\nBut in the citacao Python I called add_bdays(cit_inicio,15) with cit_inicio = protract(19/10) = 19/10. So it counted starting FROM 19/10. And then the result shown was 15 business days from 19/10 = 06/11.\n\nBut that's WRONG. The contagem should START from 20/10 (first business day after publication), and we EXCLUDE that first day (day of commencement excluded per art. 224 §1º). So effectively:\n- início contagem = 19/10? or 20/10?\n\nOK let me be very careful about the difference between \"início de contagem\" and \"exclusion of day of commencement.\"\n\nStandard CPC rule (art. 224, §1º): \"Salvo disposição em contrário, os prazos serão contados EXCLUINDO o dia do começo e Including dia do vencimento.\" So you don't count day of commencement.\n\nAnd início of the prazo (art. 224, §3º for DJe): first business day after publication.\n\nFor contests: publication = 19/10/2026. \n- início da contagem (1º útil seguinte à publicação) = 20/10/2026 (Tuesday).\n- Contagem 15 dias úteis, EXCLUINDO dia do começo (20/10): count 21/10 as day 1... day 15.\n\nAlternatively, some count the día de commence as 19/10 (the publication date) and the prazo starts 19/10, exclude 19/10, count 20/10 as day 1.\n\nBoth give the same vencimento. Let me compute: count 15 business days starting 20/10 as day 1 (exclusive method on the INÍCO), OR count starting 21/10...\n\nArgh. The cleanest: vencimento = publication + 15 business days, where publication day counts as... let me just carefully count.\n\nRule: prazo starts \"no primeiro dia útil que segue à publicação\" = 20/10/2026. Prazo counted EXCLUDING dia do começo (20/10), DIA DO VENCIMENTO INCLUDED. So:\n- 20/10 = dia do começo (excluído, day 0)\n- day 1 = 21/10 (Wed)\n- 22/10 (Thu) = 2\n- 23/10 (Fri) = 3\n- 24/10 (Sat) - skip\n- 25/10 (Sun) - skip\n- 26/10 (Mon) = 4\n- 27/10 (Tue) = 5\n- 28/10 (Wed) = 6 -- 28/10 is dia servidor público, sem expediente forense! Wait, is it?\n\nFrom the calendar: \"28/10/2026 (qua) - Dia do Servidor Público (sem expediente forense)\". So 28/10 is a NON-business day for forense purposes. It should be skipped!\n\nHmm, but in my Python I did NOT include 28/10 in the holidays set for the citacao calculation. Let me check... In the citacao add_bdays function, bd = {28/11, 25/12...}. I did NOT include 28/10. So the citacao count treated 28/10 as a normal business day. That's an error!\n\nWait, the calendar says \"28/10/2026 (qua) — Dia do Servidor Público (sem expediente forense)\". Actually re-reading: \"28/10/2026 (qua) | Dia do Servidor Público (sem expediente forense)\". Hmm, actually 28/10 is a normal holiday (Dia do Servidor Público is a legal holiday in Brazil, Lei 10.644). But the calendar note \"(sem expediente forense)\" — is it a day withoutforense expediente at all, or just within the recess context?\n\nActually, 28/10 is a national holiday (Dia do Servidor Público, Lei nº 10.644/2002) — municipalities can keep expediente but courts often close. The calendar marks it \"(sem expediente forense)\". So it IS a non-business day.\n\nBUT — for the contestação calculation, does 28/10 fall within the 15-day window? Window is 20/10 to ~09/11. 28/10 is within it. So it MUST be skipped.\n\nSo my citacao calculation (which gave 09/11 treating 28/10 as business) is WRONG. Let me recompute.\n\nActually wait. Let me recompute the contestação counting 28/10 as non-business.\n\nPublication 19/10. Início 20/10. Exclude 20/10 (day 0). Count from 20/10:\n- 20/10 (Tue) = começa (excluído)\n- 21/10 (Wed) = 1\n- 22/10 (Thu) = 2\n- 23/10 (Fri) = 3\n- 26/10 (Mon) = 4\n- 27/10 (Tue) = 5\n- 28/10 (Wed) = SKIP (dia servidor, sem expediente)\n- 29/10 (Thu) = 6\n- 30/10 (Fri) = 7\n- 02/11 (Mon) = 8\n- 03/11 (Tue) = 9\n- 04/11 (Wed) = 10\n- 05/11 (Thu) = 11\n- 06/11 (Fri) = 12\n- 09/11 (Mon) = 13\n- 10/11 (Tue) = 14\n- 11/11 (Wed) = 15 → VENCIMENTO 11/11/2026\n\nSo if 28/10 is a non-business day, vencimento = 11/11/2026, NOT 09/11/2026!\n\nHmm. So now I have THREE candidate results for contestação: 09/11 (treating 28/10 as business), 11/11 (treating 28/10 as holiday). This is exactly the kind of ambiguity I need to nail down and state.\n\nIs 28/10/2026 a holiday that suspends forense expediente? Let me look again at the calendar entry. The calendar shows several \"sem expediente forense\" entries: 28/10, and 24/12, 31/12. And it lists 02/11 (Finados), 20/11 (Zumbi), 08/12 (Conceição municipal), 20/12-20/01 recess.\n\nActually, the question is whether \"sem expediente forense\" days are excluded from day-counting. Under CPC art. 219 (dias úteis) and the calendar, days without forense expediente that are holidays/non-business are skipped. A \"Dia útil\" for processual purposes generally = day with expediente. So 28/10 (Dia do Servidor Público, sem expediente forense) IS excluded.\n\nBut wait — is that correct? Let me reconsider. The calendar explicitly labels some days \"sem expediente forense\": 18/02 (Quarta de Cinzas, \"sem prazo\"), 28/10, 24/12, 31/12. For 18/02 it says \"sem prazo\" meaning no time runs. For 28/10, \"sem expediente forense\" — a day without forense expediente = a non-business day = does not count.\n\nSo YES, 28/10 should be excluded. Let me redo everything with 28/10 counted as non-business.\n\nThis means the contestação vencimento is 11/11/2026.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider. There's genuine ambiguity: Is \"Dia do Servidor Público 28/10\" a holiday for the whole municipality or only for public offices? In Campinas, 28/10 is a municipal holiday (Lei Municipal). The calendar says \"sem expediente forense\" which strongly implies courts don't operate → non-business.\n\nSo I should exclude 28/10. Let me redo ALL calculations in one clean script, treating 28/10, 24/12, 31/12 as non-business days (they're all marked \"sem expediente forense\"), plus recess 20/12–20/01.\n\nLet me also reconsider the recess. Calendar says recess \"20/12/2026 a 20/01/2027, inclusive.\" So recess covers 20/12 to 20/01 inclusive. Business days in that window don't count.\n\nLet me redo the appeal calculation properly.\n\nSentença: publication 10/12/2026 (Thu, business). Início = 11/12 (Fri). \nContagem 15 dias úteis, exclude dia do começo (11/12)... \n\nwait, no. Let me be careful again. Início da contagem = 1º útil após publicação = 11/12. Then \"excludo dia do começo\" means 11/12 is excluded (day 0). Count from 12/12:\n- Day 1: 12/12 (Wed)... wait 12/12 — 11/12 is Friday, 12/12 is Saturday? No. 11/12/2026 — let me check. Dec 11 2026 is... Earlier script said sen_inicio = 11/12 and it's marked \"2026-12-11(Fri)\" in the apelacao contagem. So 11/12 is Friday. 12/12 is Saturday, 13/12 Sunday, 14/12 Monday.\n\nHmm wait, that means 10/12 is Thursday, 11/12 is Friday, 12/12 Saturday, 13/12 Sunday, 14/12 Monday. Goo