{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1792711601102,"sessionID":"ses_e9a22f168ffeMitJv1wz5wxyzc","part":{"id":"prt_165ef54df001LLvN5OwJLbb4fr","sessionID":"ses_e9a22f168ffeMitJv1wz5wxyzc","messageID":"msg_165ef4f32001bzBarVrHzx3xa9","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have two interpretations. Let me think about which is correct per the statutory text in the library.\n\nThe library says:\n- Art. 224 §3º: \"A contagem do prazo terá início no primeiro dia útil que seguir ao da publicação.\" → begin on the FIRST business day AFTER publication. This is the general rule for all prazos.\n- Art. 231 VII: \"considers-se dia do começo do prazo: ... VII – a data de publicação, quando a intimação se der pelo Diário da Justiça impresso ou eletrônico.\" → the START (dia do começo) is the publication date.\n\nSo there's a genuine tension. Art. 231 says the dia do começo (day of commencement) IS the publication date — that means the clock STARTS on the publication date, and under the \"include the day of commencement\" rule (224 §1), you'd count inclusively.\n\nArt. 224 §3º says the count begins the first day after publication — meaning you exclude the publication date.\n\nHmm, this is a real ambiguity in the CPC. Let me think about it more carefully in actual Brazilian civil procedure.\n\nActually, in the actual CPC and prevailing interpretation (STJ jurisprudence), the rule for publication/diary is: \n- Art. 224 §3º and art. 231 VII together establish the \"double rule\" — the dia do começo is the date of publication, BUT the count begins the first useful day AFTER publication (this is the widely applied \"regra do dobro\" — \"prazo em dobro\"). \n\nActually the canonical rule is: \"O termo inicial é o primeiro dia útil seguinte à data da publicação, a contar do qual se contarão os dias úteis consecutivos.\" This is the standard applied to DJe publications. STJ has consistently held that for publications in the DJe, the count starts on the first business day following the publication.\n\nSo interpretation (A) is the correct/prevalent one: 15 days from 14/12, embargos 5 days from 17/12, recuso 15 days from 02/02.\n\nBut wait — 231 VII clearly states \"considera-se dia do começo do prazo: VII — a data de publicação.\" So the dia do começo IS the publication date. Then 224 §1 says count from dia do começo, excluing dia do começo? No — 224 §1 says \"excluindo o dia do começo e incluindo o dia do vencimento.\" So if dia do começo = publication date (19/11 or 10/12), we EXCLUDE that day and count 15 business days after → result = interpretation (A) result.\n\nActually that reconciles it! Let me re-examine:\n- Art. 231 VII: the dia do começo (day of commencement) = the publication date (19/11/2026). For citação/DJe.\n- Art. 224 §1º: count excluí o dia do começo, inclui o dia do vencimento.\n- Art. 224 §2º: data de publicação = 1º dia útil seguinte à disponibilização.\n- Art. 224 §3º: contagem terá início no primeiro dia útil que seguir à publicação.\n\nSo reading together: dia do começo = publication date. Contagem starts counting business days AFTER that. Result: 15 business days after 19/11/2026 = 14/12/2026.\n\nThat's interpretation (A). Both Art. 224 §3º AND Art. 231 VII + Art. 224 §1º give the SAME result — the \"count starting from the day after publication\" model.\n\nSo why would (B) ever happen? Interpretation (B) would be if someone counts the publication date AS the first day counted (inclusive). But the statute says excluí o dia do começo (exclude the day of commencement). So (A) is correct.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. There are two sub-rules:\n1. Art. 224 §2º: \"Considera-se como data de publicação o primeiro dia útil seguinte ao da disponibilização\" — publication date = first useful day after disponibilização.\n2. Art. 229... no.\n\nHmm wait. Actually looking again at the CPC text:\n- §2º: publicacao = 1º util seguinte à disponibilização.\n- §3º: contagem terá início no primeiro dia útil que seguir ao considerado como data de publicação.\n\nFor our dates, disponibilização (Thu) → publicação (same Thu, already useful) → contagem starts (from first useful day AFTER publicação) = Friday 20/11. \n\nBut earlier calc.py showed inicio = 23/11 (Monday), skipping Fri 20/11. Why? Because Friday 20/11 — is it excluded? 20/11/2026 is NOT in NONBUS in calc.py! Let me check: NONBUS in calc.py includes 2026-11-20. But the actual list I derived says 20/11/2026 is NOT a holiday (only 21/04, 09/07, 20/11, ... wait let me re-check).\n\nHold on. Let me re-read the calendar. The holidays in the calendar:\n- 20/11/2026 (sex) — Dia Nacional de Zumbi e da Consciência Negra\n\nSo 20/11/2026 IS a holiday! I need to include it. But the calendar lists it and my NONBUS didn't have the date right... wait, I did add 2026-11-20 to the SECOND calc script. But calc.py (version 3) has it too. Let me check: calc.py NONBUS set includes datetime.date(2026, 11, 20)? Let me look at the top of calc.py.\n\nActually, wait — the issue is that in calc.py, the FIRST line I added was datetime.date(2026, 10, 24) which is a Saturday and irrelevant. Then datetime.date(2026, 11, 20). Let me check if 11/20 is there.\n\nFrom the file output earlier:\n```\ndatetime.date(2026, 10, 24),  # example none; will populate real ones below\ndatetime.date(2026, 11, 20),  # Dia Nacional de Zumbi e Consciência Negra (Lei 14.759/2023)\n```\n\nWait no — that was from my NEW script I wrote inline with the heredoc. Let me look at what calc.py actually contains. The file shown at lines 8-30 starts at datetime.date(2026, 10, 24) but that's not the beginning. Let me look at the full calc.py.\n\nActually, 20/11/2026 IS the day after publication (Thu 19/11 → Fri 20/11). If 20/11 is a holiday (Dia Nacional de Zumbi), then the first useful day after 19/11 would be 23/11 (Monday). That matches calc.py's inicio=23/11! Good. So calc.py correctly excluded 20/11.\n\nBut wait — is 20/11/2026 really a holiday per the calendar? Let me check the calendar again: yes: \"2\n","metadata":{