blicação\", that means contagem starts counting from 20/11. And art 224 §1 says exclude dia do começo (20/11) and count.\n\nHmm, but actually the standard interpretation is: the start day (dia do começo) = 20/11. Count business days AFTER start day. So business days after 20/11:\n23/11 (1), 24/11 (2), 25/11 (3), 26/11 (4), 27/11 (5), 30/11 (6), 01/12 (7), 02/12 (8), 03/12 (9), 04/12 (10), 07/12 (11), 08/12 (12), 09/12 (13), 10/12 (14), 11/12 (15).\n\nSo vence em 11/12/2026! \n\nBut calc3.py gave 14/12. Let me look at why.\n\nIn calc3.py, count_days(start, n): first counted day = start (inclusive), skip non-business.\nc_inicio = next_bday_after(19/11) = 20/11.\ncount_days(20/11, 15): counts 20/11 as day 1!\n1. 20/11 (Fri)  ← INCORRECT - this includes the start day as a counted day\n2. 23/11\n...\n\nSo calc3.py INCORRECTLY counted 20/11 (the start day) as one of the 15 days. That's the bug. The start day should be EXCLUDED.\n\nWait, so which is correct? Let me be careful about the mechanics.\n\nActually, there are TWO different \"start day\" concepts that get confusingly conflated:\n\nInterpretation A (art 224 §3 + §1): \n- dia do começo/contagem = primeiro dia útil seguinte ao da publicação = 20/11\n- Contagem: exclude dia do começo (20/11), 15 diasutos subsequentes → vence 11/12\n\nInterpretation (my first calc.py):\n- inicio_contagem = next_bday_after(pub) = 20/11 is the start\n- Then add_bdays(start_exclusive=20/11,...) excludes 20/11 and counts 15 days after → 14/12\n\nWait, add_bdays(20/11, 15) where start is EXCLUSIVE:\nd starts at 20/11. Loop increments first: 21/11 (Sat, skip), 22/11 (Sun, skip), 23/11 (Mon, count 1), 24 (2), 25(3), 26(4), 27(5), 30/11(6), 01/12(7), 02(8), 03(9), 04(10), 07(11), 08(12), 09(13), 10(14), 11(15). → 11/12!\n\nWait that gives 11/12, not 14/12. But calc.py printed 14/12! Let me re-examine.\n\nLooking at calc.py output again:\n```\ninicio contagem (art. 224o3 / 231VII): Monday 23/11/2026\n```\n\nIt said inicio = Monday 23/11, not 20