lly has the right number (14/12) but the table shows days wrong. Let me fix the table.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety. Earlier I noted the bug in count_days where it included the start day. But that gave [feriado] tag on 20/11, implying 20/11 was counted as day 1. If 20/11 is excluded because it's a holiday, then... \n\nUgh, I'm getting confused by the different bug behaviors. Let me just carefully rewrite ONE clean script with correct mechanics and NO ambiguity. Let me define it very explicitly and print day-by-day for me to verify by hand.\n\nThe critical facts:\n- Publication citacao = 19/11/2026 (Thursday)\n- 20/11/2026 = Friday = Dia Nacional de Zumbi (holiday) — confirmed by calendar\n- 21-22/11 = Saturday/Sunday\n- 23/11/2026 = Monday (first business day after publication)\n- 28/11/2026 = Dia do Servidor Público (holiday, but also Saturday)\n- 10/12/2026 = Thursday (sentença disponibilidade)\n- 20/12/2026 onwards = recessão (art. 220) — until 20/01/2027\n- 24/12/2026, 31/12/2026, 01/01/2027 — sem expediente/holiday\n\nLet me write a clean calculation. I'll define the \"count\" as: starting from the início da contagem (first business day after publication), the day of início is EXCLUDED, count N business days, last one is vencimento.\n\nActually — I realize I should double check whether the traditional rule here is \"start = 23/11, count 15 days starting FROM 23/11 inclusive\" or \"exclude 23/11, count from 24/11\". \n\nThe two interpretations:\n- Interpretation 1 (strict 224 §1): dia do começo = 23/11 (the start), EXCLUDE it, count 15 after → 14/12\n- Interpretation 2: some practitioners count 23/11 as day 1.\n\nGiven the well-known rule \"exclui o dia do começo e inclui o do vencimento\" (dia-a-dia, one in one out), and that art 231 VII says \"considera-se dia do começo do prazo: ... VII – a data de publicação\" — but then art 224 §3 says contagem inicia no 1º útil seguinte. These are reconciled as: the dia do começo is the 1º útil seguinte (23/11), and art